# Engineering Workflow

Choose the workflow by the work, not by habit.

- Use `/ship` for a bounded, well-understood change that can be fully specified
  up front and run without user interaction.
- Use Plan Mode and a durable `docs/worklogs/<feature-slug>.md` for a large or
  ambiguous feature, cross-cutting change, or work that needs decisions at phase
  gates. Record `intake -> discovery -> planned -> implementing -> testing ->
  reviewing -> iterating -> complete`, including transitions, evidence, and
  unresolved questions.
- A written plan can be executed directly, but it does not replace discovery
  when requirements or repository behavior remain uncertain.

Treat ambiguity that affects implementation as blocking. Ask for a decision or
record a clearly bounded assumption before proceeding; do not invent behavior.

Inspect the repository before editing. Keep changes focused, follow established
conventions, and do not revert unrelated worktree changes. Test the changed
behavior and run the narrowest relevant verification after the final edit.

Before pushing, check whether the current branch has an open PR. After every
push to an open PR, refresh its description from the current diff, including
the implementation summary, review order, and actual test commands. Never
force-push a shared branch.
